<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Preparing for Exam 2</title>

  <p component="prod-04">
    Our first exam is <alert>Wednesday, November 18</alert> during
    our usual class meeting time.  We will take some time on Monday,
    November 16 to review a bit, but it would be a good idea to start
    earlier.
  </p>

  <p component="prod-02">
    Our first exam is <alert>Thursday, November 19</alert> during
    our usual class meeting time.  We will take some time on Tuesday,
    November 17 to review a bit, but it would be a good idea to start
    earlier.
  </p>

  <p>
    <alert>Technology:</alert>  You will be allowed to use a calculator
    but not Desmos or other resources.
  </p>
  
  <paragraphs>
    <title>Topics</title>

    <p>
      The exam will focus on everything that we've done in the course
      after the first exam up until now.  One of the best things to do
      is to work through the in-class activities and make sure that
      you feel comfortable with everything.  Solutions to all of those
      activities are available on Blackboard, but you can also print
      new copies using the printer icon and work through them again.
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>Remember:</alert> The most important thing is for you to
      focus on concepts.  It's also great to find an answer, but be
      sure that you understand all the steps that you've taken to get
      there and how they fit together.  If you have this kind of
      understanding, you should be able to deal with situations that
      may be slightly new.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here is a list of topics that we have studied.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            For the final <q>slice-compute-add</q> topics, we
            considered
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Some topics in finance such as computing the future-
                  and present-value of an income stream.  Rather than
                  memorize a formula, you should understand what we
                  did and why it works.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Some topics in probability such as
                  <ul>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        Probability density functions:  what is a
                        probability density function, what kind of
                        information does it tell us, and how can we
                        recognize a probability density function?
                      </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        Cumulative distribution functions:  what is a
                        cumulative distribution function, how it
                        related to a probability density function, and
                        what are some of properties of a cumulative
                        distribution function?
                      </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        Some summary statistics, such as
                        <ul>
                          <li><p>mean</p></li>
                          <li><p>median</p></li>
                          <li><p>and how they are related to one
                          another</p></li>
                        </ul>
                      </p>
                    </li>
                  </ul>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Differential equations
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  How do differential equations arise?  One thing
                  that's important that some people have found
                  challenging is to look at a situation and describe
                  it using a differential equation.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What is an equilibrium solution and what does it
                  mean to be stable or unstable?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  How can we graphically understand autonomous
                  equations by sketching a plot of <m>dy/dt</m> versus
                  <m>y</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What is a slope field?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What does Euler's method do and how does it work?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  How can we solve a separable differential equation?
                  What is the role of <m>+C</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Time</title>

    <p>
      Just a reminder:  I realize that in-class exams can be stressful
      because of the time constraints.  I take this seriously and will
      design the exam so that you can easily complete it in our class
      meeting (one hour, fifty minutes).  Your job is to make sure you
      are prepared.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

</worksheet>
